#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Create or reuse a Vitis Unified Versal PLM workspace, overlay custom sources,
build plm.elf, optionally build an RPU application, and generate the launch PDI.

Examples
--------

First run:

    vitis -s build-plm.py \
        ../plm_vitis_project \
        --xsa ../system.xsa \
        --custom-source-dir ../example-user-module/src \
        --symlink-sources

Subsequent runs using the existing platform and PLM component:

    vitis -s build-plm.py \
        ../plm_vitis_project \
        --custom-source-dir ../example-user-module/src \
        --symlink-sources

Force complete regeneration:

    vitis -s build-plm.py \
        ../plm_vitis_project \
        --xsa ../system.xsa \
        --custom-source-dir ../example-user-module/src \
        --symlink-sources \
        --force-regenerate

Build the PLM IPI user module and its R5-0 peer application:

    vitis -s build-plm.py \
        ../plm_vitis_project \
        --xsa ../system.xsa \
        --custom-source-dir ./src \
        --register-module \
            xplm_ipi_ping_pong_module.h:XPlm_IpiPingPongModuleInit \
        --user-modules-count 1 \
        --rpu-source ./rpu-app \
        --symlink-sources \
        --force-regenerate \
        --launch-vitis

By default, the Vitis launch configurations are patched for attach-to-running
debug. The PLM and RPU ELFs are used for symbols/source mapping while firmware
is loaded by the PDI, JTAG, or remoteproc flow.

Pass --embed-rpu-in-pdi to additionally generate a production-style combined
PDI that boots both the PLM and RPU without the debugger. The combined PDI is
kept separate from the PLM-only PDI used by the Vitis debug launch.
"""

